# Tailwind CSS + daisyUI

This project was generated with `css=tailwind`: pages are styled with Tailwind utility classes and [daisyUI](https://daisyui.com) component classes instead of the hand-written stylesheet the `css=vanilla` option would have used. This skill covers how *this project's* setup works — the Bun-based build, the daisyUI theme config, which classes this project actually reaches for — not the whole Tailwind/daisyUI reference (there are hundreds of utilities and dozens of components; reach for them as you need them, same as you'd search any CSS framework's docs).

## When to reach for this

- Styling a new page or partial, or reaching for a daisyUI component (`btn`, `card`, `input`, `alert`, ...) instead of hand-composing utility classes.
- Adding a new design token, or changing the light/dark theme.
- A freshly generated (or freshly cloned) project has **no styling at all** — this is expected, see Quick reference below, not a bug.
- A daisyUI class doesn't seem to do anything, or doesn't look like daisyUI at all.
- Deciding whether a dependency belongs in `pyproject.toml` or `package.json`.

## Quick reference

- **Two toolchains, two dependency files**: Python dependencies stay in `pyproject.toml` (`uv sync`); frontend dependencies (`tailwindcss`, `@tailwindcss/cli`, `daisyui`) live in `package.json`, installed with [Bun](https://bun.sh) (`bun install`) — `uv add`/`uv sync` never touch `package.json`, and `bun add`/`bun install` never touch `pyproject.toml`. This project uses Bun specifically (not plain `npm`/`node`) — it's the JS runtime *and* the package manager, one tool instead of two.
- **One command runs everything while developing**: `uv run dev.py` starts the {% if framework == "fastapi" %}FastAPI{% else %}Flask{% endif %} dev server and the Tailwind watcher together (`bun run watch:css` under the hood) — see the {% if framework == "fastapi" %}`fastapi`{% else %}`flask`{% endif %} skill for what the dev server itself does; this skill covers the CSS half. Requires `bun install` to have been run at least once first.
- **Config lives in CSS, not JS — for both Tailwind and daisyUI**: `src/{{ package_name }}/static/css/input.css` is just:
  ```css
  @import "tailwindcss";
  @plugin "daisyui" {
    themes: light --default, dark --prefersdark;
  }
  ```
  No `tailwind.config.js` (Tailwind v4 is CSS-first) and no separate daisyUI config file — `themes: light --default, dark --prefersdark` picks daisyUI's built-in `light` theme by default and switches to `dark` automatically based on the OS/browser's `prefers-color-scheme`, matching how the `css=vanilla` variant's plain CSS already behaves. See `references/theming-and-components.md` for adding more themes or custom colors.
- **You have to build it — nothing watches automatically outside `dev.py`**: `input.css` is the source; `static/css/style.css` (the file `base.html` actually links) is generated output, gitignored, and **does not exist until you build it once**:
  ```
  bun run build:css
  ```
  `bun run watch:css` rebuilds on every save (this is what `uv run dev.py` already runs for you). If `--docker` was also chosen, the Dockerfile builds the CSS automatically in a separate `oven/bun` build stage — that combination needs no manual step, and the final image never contains `node_modules` or the Bun toolchain itself.
- **No content config needed**: Tailwind v4's CLI scans the project directory for class names automatically (no `content: [...]` array to keep in sync, unlike Tailwind v3) — a class typed in any `.html` file under this project is picked up the next time you build, with nothing extra to configure.
- **Prefer daisyUI component classes over hand-composed utilities**: `templates/index.html` and `templates/partials/*.html` use `btn btn-primary`, `input input-bordered`, `card`/`card-body`/`card-title`, `checkbox checkbox-primary` rather than assembling the same look from raw utilities — see `references/theming-and-components.md` for the classes this project actually uses, and `references/daisyui-components.md` for the other 80+ components daisyUI ships (alerts, modals, dropdowns, navbars, ...) before adding any new UI.

